meta { title = "GPU numerical graphs", description = "Compose resident numerical graphs with strict types and shapes.", order = 3, group = "HOWTO guides" }

Once you can run the first GPU example, combine numerical operations inside a function placed on that device. Keep intermediate tensors on the GPU and download only the values the CPU needs. The compiler may use several kernels or submissions for one function.

The examples below use the Metal commands shown on this page. For Vulkan or CUDA, use the corresponding backend commands and prerequisites in the GPU guide.

list {
  link { url = "/docs/gpu", "First GPU program and backend-specific build commands" }
}

h2 { "Row math for attention" }

transpose, softmaxRows, and rmsNormRows compose with matrix multiplication inside numerical GPU programs. Transpose preserves bits for all ten numeric types. The two row-math operations currently require hardware f32 on Metal and experimental Vulkan/CUDA; their CPU implementations also support hardware f64. RMS normalization takes an explicit finite, positive epsilon of the same type as its tensor. It has unit gain. These operations do not move data between CPU and GPU implicitly.

codeblock { language = kalandra, """from "std/io" import println, IoError
from "std/fmt" import format

fn normalize(scores: Tensor[f32, ?, ?]) -> Tensor[f32, ?, ?]:
  return softmaxRows(scores)

fn main() -> unit ! (TaskError | IoError):
  output: f32 = try parallel:
    gpu = devices.gpu()
    cpu = devices.cpu()
    scores: Tensor[f32, 2, 3] = tensor([[1, 2, 3], [-1, 0, 1]])
    a = try await transfer(scores, gpu)
    b = try await spawn normalize(a) on gpu
    result = try await transfer(b, cpu)
    reduceSum(result)

  try println(format("{}", output))
  return ()
""" }

The numerical result is approximately 2: each row sums to approximately one. Intermediate outputs stay alive until the device has finished using them. Composing operations avoids unnecessary transfers, but does not guarantee a single kernel or command submission. Softmax uses row maxima for stability and an approximation to the exponential function; CPU and GPU results need not be bit-identical.

h2 { "Compose two matrix products" }

codeblock { language = kalandra, """from "std/io" import print, println, IoError
from "std/fmt" import format

fn multiply(a: Tensor[f32, ?, ?], b: Tensor[f32, ?, ?]) -> Tensor[f32, ?, ?]:
  return matmul(a, b)

fn project(input: Tensor[f32, ?, ?], weights: Tensor[f32, ?, ?], projection: Tensor[f32, ?, ?]) -> Tensor[f32, ?, ?]:
  hidden = multiply(input, weights)
  return multiply(hidden, projection)

fn main() -> unit ! (TaskError | IoError):
  output: Tensor[f32, ?, ?] = try parallel:
    gpu = devices.gpu()
    input: Tensor[f32, 2, 3] = tensor([[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]])
    weights: Tensor[f32, 3, 2] = tensor([[1, 2], [3, 4], [5, 6]])
    projection: Tensor[f32, 2, 2] = tensor([[1, 2], [3, 4]])
    a = try await transfer(input, gpu)
    b = try await transfer(weights, gpu)
    c = try await transfer(projection, gpu)
    work = spawn project(a, b, c) on gpu
    result = try await work
    cached = try await work
    try await transfer(cached, devices.cpu())

  outputValues = array(output)
  try print("[")
  for index in range(length(outputValues)):
    if index > 0:
      try print(", ")
    try print(format("{}", outputValues[index]))
  try println("]")
  return ()
""" }

Save the complete example as main.k and run it with the mixed CPU/Metal toolchain:

codeblock { language = bash, """kalandra main.k --target metal -O2 -o app
./app/program
""" }

The numerical result is the row-major matrix [[106, 156], [241, 354]]. The host output loop displays [106, 156, 241, 354] in row-major order.

The two ordinary helper calls form a resident graph. In this example the input is 2 by 3, weights are 3 by 2, and projection is 2 by 2. The final result is 2 by 2. Awaiting work twice reuses the completed tensor; only the final explicit transfer downloads it.

h2 { "Prepare inputs with arrays" }

Use numeric and bool arrays to build and edit data on the CPU side of a GPU program. Local reassignment, map, generate, copy, sorting and element writes follow the same alias and task-reader rules as CPU programs. CPU helpers can receive and return arrays, including across task waits.

Convert a numeric array to a tensor before transferring it to a GPU. tensor(values) copies the data, so later array edits cannot change the tensor snapshot or a running GPU calculation.

codeblock { language = kalandra, """from "std/io" import println, IoError
from "std/fmt" import format

fn twice(values: Tensor[f32, ?]) -> Tensor[f32, ?]:
  return values + values

fn main() -> unit ! (TaskError | IoError):
  output: f32 = try parallel:
    values = generate(3, fn(index): return f32(index + 1))
    snapshot = tensor(values)
    values[0] = f32(9)
    gpu = devices.gpu()
    resident = try await transfer(snapshot, gpu)
    work = spawn twice(resident) on gpu
    result = try await transfer(try await work, devices.cpu())
    reduceSum(result) + values[0]

  try println(format("{}", output))
  return ()
""" }

This displays 21. The GPU doubles the original [1, 2, 3] snapshot; the CPU adds the edited first array element, 9. array(tensor) also copies, so editing that array cannot change the tensor.

This example prepares arrays on the host and transfers an immutable tensor snapshot. GPU helpers can also construct and modify their own numeric/bool arrays, as described below. Flat numeric and bool arrays can also cross the task boundary as read-only inputs or as results. Arrays of Strings or function values and parallelMap are not supported as coordinating values in mixed GPU programs. Eligible records, enums and CPU-created function values can be passed to GPU tasks as described below.

h2 { "Types and ranks" }

The supported numerical types are i8, u8, i16, u16, i32, u32, i64, u64, f32 and f64. Dense tensors of rank zero through eight are supported by the numerical operations that accept those ranks. matmul remains rank two. Numeric identity and complete shape obligations are checked rather than implicitly promoted or broadcast.

h2 { "Hardware arithmetic and explicit fallback" }

Float32 uses hardware arithmetic on CPU, Metal and the experimental Vulkan/CUDA backends by default. CUDA also uses native f64 on supported hardware. Integer arithmetic retains overflow checks. Apple Metal has no native f64 arithmetic on the supported target; compiling f64 GPU arithmetic requires the explicit option --arithmetic hardware-with-software-fallback. Under that policy, CPU f64 and GPU f32 stay on hardware, while GPU f64 uses integer-based software arithmetic on the GPU.

Use --arithmetic software for the strict software implementation on both CPU and GPU. This selects software arithmetic with gradual underflow and its defined numerical contract. Default hardware mode follows device behavior, so Metal f32 subnormals may flush to zero. reduceSum keeps its balanced adjacent-pair tree, and scalar arithmetic retains its separately rounded operations. -O2 does not enable generic fast math.

h2 { "Matrix precision policies" }

Metal hardware f32 matmul defaults to optimized full-precision accumulation. Experimental Vulkan and CUDA default to fixed-tree and require explicit --matmul-policy optimized for their tiled kernels. The compiler may use fused multiply-add and choose a contraction order suited to the device while preserving f32 inputs and accumulators. This does not silently convert inputs to float16 or TF32.

For eligible aligned f32 products, CUDA's optimized path splits the contraction into five independent GPU partitions and reduces them deterministically. It keeps full f32 inputs and accumulators. Temporary workspace counts against the device budget; if that workspace cannot fit, a slower GPU route computes the same accumulation order. Memory pressure does not select a different numerical result. Unsupported shapes keep the fixed-tree GPU companion.

Use --matmul-policy fixed-tree to require separately rounded products and the existing balanced contraction tree. This selects a calculation order, not exact real arithmetic or universally greater accuracy. Optimized results may differ in rounding, signed zero and intermediate overflow. Subtraction of nearly equal large terms, called numerical cancellation, can lose accuracy in either mode; it is unrelated to task cancellation. Checked integer operations are unchanged, and CPU matmul currently retains the fixed tree under either policy.

codeblock { language = bash, """kalandra main.k --target metal --matmul-policy fixed-tree -O2 -o app
./app/program
""" }

Hardware fixed-tree mode still follows the device's underflow and NaN behavior. Use --arithmetic software when the software numerical contract, including gradual underflow, is required; that option always selects fixed-tree matmul.

h2 { "Conditions in GPU functions" }

Eligible GPU functions can use numeric comparisons, Boolean expressions and conditions to choose work. Only a selected branch runs, including its shape checks. Boolean conditions do not make bool tensors, Strings or arbitrary host objects valid device data. Device and task resource limits still apply.

GPU functions can accept bool scalars and return bool results. Awaiting a bool task produces a bool, not a numeric byte; numeric and bool arguments are not interchangeable. Formatting a bool displays true or false, and the host formats numerical results in decimal.

codeblock { language = kalandra, """from "std/io" import println, IoError
from "std/fmt" import format

fn oddAndEnabled(enabled: bool, value: i64) -> bool:
  return enabled and value % 2 == 1

fn main() -> unit ! (TaskError | IoError):
  output: bool = try parallel:
    gpu = devices.gpu()
    result = spawn oddAndEnabled(true, 7) on gpu
    first = try await result
    second = try await result
    first and second

  try println(format("{}", output))
  return ()
""" }

This prints true. Repeated awaits observe the same completed value. The same bool result type works for CPU tasks and for coordinating helpers that await GPU work. Bounds/arithmetic failures and cancellation remain task failures; they are not converted into false.

h2 { "Scalar loops on the GPU" }

Numeric and bool scalar helpers can use local numeric and bool state, while loops, for loops over range(...), break and continue on the GPU. Both single-result loop expressions and statement loops are supported. Call these helpers from numerical GPU functions or place a numeric or bool scalar entry directly on the device.

codeblock { language = kalandra, """from "std/io" import println, IoError
from "std/fmt" import format

fn countSteps(count: i64) -> f32:
  total: f32 = 0
  for index in range(count):
    total = total + f32(1)
  return total

fn main() -> unit ! (TaskError | IoError):
  output: f32 = try parallel:
    gpu = devices.gpu()
    try await spawn countSteps(1000) on gpu

  try println(format("{}", output))
  return ()
""" }

This computes 1000 on the GPU. The host println call displays 1000. Loop arithmetic and branch decisions execute on the device. Each scalar helper runs sequentially in one GPU thread; placing it on a GPU does not parallelize its iterations. The runtime saves private scalar state between bounded chunks and checks cancellation before submitting more work. There is no total iteration limit; execution remains subject to ordinary device, task and memory limits. A cancellation request cannot reclaim buffers until submitted work has finished.

GPU helpers can carry numeric and bool values, supported arrays, records, enums and numeric tensors across iterations. Direct and mutually recursive calls can carry supported values across calls. Each active call uses invocation-owned device storage subject to the task memory budget. Numeric policies still apply; Metal f64 arithmetic requires explicit software arithmetic or hardware-with-software-fallback.

h2 { "Local arrays and collecting loops" }

Inside a GPU helper, materialize a yielding expression immediately with array(for ... yield ...). Stored Iterator values and lazy adapters run on the CPU host; transfer their completed numerical data before launching a GPU task.

A GPU helper can construct numeric and bool arrays, index and iterate them, and modify local elements. Assignment keeps the same backing; copy creates independent element storage. slice and concat also construct independent arrays. A synchronous helper with a mut parameter can modify an array created by the calling GPU helper. GPU tasks can capture existing numeric and bool arrays as read-only parameters. Helpers can return flat numeric or bool arrays as native CPU arrays after await.

codeblock { language = kalandra, """from "std/io" import println, IoError
from "std/fmt" import format

fn selected(n: i64) -> i64:
  indices = generate(n, fn(i): return i)
  values = array(for i in indices:
    if i % 2 == 1:
      for offset in range(2):
        yield i * 10 + offset
  )
  snapshot = copy(values)
  values[0] = 999
  total = 0
  ordered = sorted(map(snapshot, fn(value): return value))
  for value in ordered:
    total = total + value
  return total

fn main() -> unit ! (TaskError | IoError):
  output: i64 = try parallel:
    gpu = devices.gpu()
    try await spawn selected(10) on gpu

  try println(format("{}", output))
  return ()
""" }

This computes 505 and prints its i64 bits as 505. yield appends in lexical order; filtering, nested loops, break, continue and empty results keep their ordinary CPU semantics. Array operations and collection run on the GPU, sequentially within the helper invocation. These arrays do not become shared append buffers or implicitly parallel loops.

generate calls a callback with each index; array map calls it with each input element. Both visit indices in increasing order and allocate independent output storage before callbacks run. Named nonrecursive helpers and callback literals with supported captured values run on-device, including callbacks that use loops and local arrays. An empty input makes no callback calls; a negative generate count produces a checked bounds fault.

Local arrays use invocation-owned device memory. Their backing can grow as needed, subject to device and task memory budgets. Unreachable numeric/bool arrays are reclaimed during execution, so repeatedly constructing temporary arrays does not retain every earlier allocation. Allocation, copying and collection have cancellation checkpoints. Checked bounds errors discard partial results, and submitted work settles before its storage is released.

The numeric and bool array representation uses a 64-bit cell per element plus private allocation metadata. Memory costs can therefore exceed the corresponding CPU array payload. Eligible record arrays and device-local closure arrays can also live in the invocation arena. Strings remain host-only. sort orders a local numeric/bool array in place, so aliases observe the changes. sorted allocates independent backing first. Both use the same deterministic order as CPU arrays, including NaNs and signed zero, and compare floating values by integer keys without floating-point arithmetic. Sorting has cancellation checkpoints between bounded comparison/swap steps.

h2 { "Records and enums in GPU helpers" }

A device helper can construct records and enum variants containing numeric scalars, bool, numeric/bool arrays and nested records. Field access, record updates, value copies and match execute on the device, including inside loops. Internal nonrecursive helpers can accept and return those values.

codeblock { language = kalandra, """from "std/io" import println, IoError
from "std/fmt" import format

type Sample:
  value: f32
  enabled: bool

fn adjusted(value: f32) -> f32:
  sample = Sample {value: value, enabled: true}
  return if sample.enabled:
    sample.value + f32(2)
  else:
    f32(0)

fn main() -> unit ! (TaskError | IoError):
  output: f32 = try parallel:
    gpu = devices.gpu()
    try await spawn adjusted(f32(40)) on gpu

  try println(format("{}", output))
  return ()
""" }

This computes 42; the executable prints 42. Records are copied by value within the private device frame. Inactive enum payloads are zeroed, and fields retain their declared numeric types. Record array fields preserve their backing aliases. Use copy on an array field when you need independent element storage. Eligible records can also cross the task boundary as described below. Device record fields support numeric and bool scalars, flat numeric and bool arrays, and nested records or enums of those types.

codeblock { language = kalandra, """from "std/io" import println, IoError
from "std/fmt" import format

type Batch:
  values: [i64]

fn sumBatch(count: i64) -> i64:
  batch = Batch {values: generate(count, fn(index): return index + 1)}
  total = 0
  for value in batch.values:
    total = total + value
  return total

fn main() -> unit ! (TaskError | IoError):
  output: i64 = try parallel:
    gpu = devices.gpu()
    try await spawn sumBatch(4) on gpu

  try println(format("{}", output))
  return ()
""" }

This computes 10 and prints 10. The array is constructed and read on the GPU. Its backing remains alive while batch refers to it, including across helper calls and allocation checkpoints.

h2 { "Capture host arrays" }

Pass a numeric or bool array directly to a GPU-placed helper. The task captures the array as read-only, packs and uploads its elements, then executes the helper on the GPU. Packing belongs to the spawned task; spawn does not wait for it. The original array and its aliases stay read-locked until the task settles. An empty array is valid.

codeblock { language = kalandra, """from "std/io" import println, IoError
from "std/fmt" import format

fn firstAndOriginal(values: [i64]) -> i64:
  ordered = sorted(values)
  return ordered[0] + values[0]

fn main() -> unit ! (TaskError | IoError):
  values: [i64] = [5, 3, 1]
  output: i64 = try parallel:
    gpu = devices.gpu()
    try await spawn firstAndOriginal(values) on gpu

  try println(format("{}", output))
  return ()
""" }

This computes 6 and prints 6. sorted creates independent device storage; values remains unchanged. Captured arrays follow the same read-only rules as ordinary function parameters. Use copy or sorted before modifying elements inside the GPU helper. Writing through a host alias while the task is pending is a compile-time error.

Array capture supports all ten numeric types and bool, subject to the selected arithmetic policy. Packing and upload costs count toward end-to-end latency, and their storage counts toward task and device budgets. The private wire representation uses a 64-bit cell per element, so it can use more memory than the corresponding CPU array. A GPU entry can accept captured host arrays alongside tensors already resident on the selected device. GPU tasks can return supported scalars, arrays, records, enums and tensors. Array and record results materialize on the CPU. Tensor results remain on the GPU.

h2 { "Return arrays from GPU tasks" }

A GPU helper can return a numeric or bool array. Awaiting it gives the caller a native CPU array. The helper's computation runs on the GPU; returning its array includes device-to-host transfer and decoding costs. Empty results are valid, and their element type comes from the function's result type.

codeblock { language = kalandra, """from "std/io" import println, IoError
from "std/fmt" import format

fn ordered(values: [i64]) -> [i64]:
  return sorted(values)

fn main() -> unit ! (TaskError | IoError):
  values: [i64] = [9, 2, 5]
  output: i64 = try parallel:
    gpu = devices.gpu()
    result = try await spawn ordered(values) on gpu
    result[0] = 7
    result[0] + values[0]

  try println(format("{}", output))
  return ()
""" }

This computes 16 and prints 16. The GPU sorts into independent storage, so editing result leaves values unchanged. A helper returning a captured array directly preserves its original CPU backing instead: assignment and helper calls do not imply a copy. Other pending readers still prevent writes through any alias. Use copy or sorted when you need independent backing.

Generators do not need an input array: a GPU helper can return the array produced by a for/while expression or generate. Result order follows the corresponding CPU operation. A returned array can be read, modified under the normal access rules, or captured by another GPU task. For repeated calculations on resident tensors, use explicit tensor transfers instead of moving an array back after each task.

main can return the array directly:

codeblock { language = kalandra, """from "std/io" import print, println, IoError
from "std/fmt" import format

fn squares(n: i64) -> [i64]:
  return generate(n, fn(i): return i * i)

fn main() -> unit ! (TaskError | IoError):
  output: [i64] = try parallel:
    gpu = devices.gpu()
    try await spawn squares(4) on gpu

  try print("[")
  for index in range(length(output)):
    if index > 0:
      try print(", ")
    try print(format("{}", output[index]))
  try println("]")
  return ()
""" }

The values are [0, 1, 4, 9]. The host loop formats each element in decimal; bool elements use true and false.


Input packing, temporary device storage, result transfer and native result storage count toward their respective resource budgets. Transfers and decoding proceed in bounded pages with cancellation checks. A bounds fault, allocation failure or cancellation discards partial results; submitted copies settle before their backing is released.

h2 { "AI and LLM foundations" }

Matrix products, tensor arithmetic, conversions and reductions are numerical building blocks for AI workloads. Kalandra does not supply model loading, tokenizers or a full LLM inference runtime, and the Linux CUDA backend remains experimental. Start with a small numerical graph and measure both resident execution and transfer-inclusive latency.

h2 { "Records in mixed CPU and GPU code" }

CPU helpers and CPU tasks can receive and return records and enums while the program also runs GPU work. Their arrays keep normal alias identity, including when returned through a CPU task. Recursive links, String fields and record-array fields can remain in these CPU values. Writes through an array field or its alias are rejected while a task is reading it; an independent copy remains writable.

GPU tasks can capture a whole record or enum when its fields are numeric or bool scalars, flat numeric or bool arrays, or nested records and enums of those types. Only the selected enum payload is used. Captured arrays remain read-only and preserve their aliases. These helpers accept additional scalar or flat-array parameters. They can return a scalar, a flat array, or a whole record or enum of the same supported field types. After await, returned arrays are native CPU arrays. Fields sharing fresh backing remain aliases, and returned captured arrays still alias their original backing.

codeblock { language = kalandra, """from "std/io" import println, IoError
from "std/fmt" import format

type Batch:
  values: [i64]
  bias: i64

fn sumBatch(batch: Batch) -> i64:
  total = 0
  for value in batch.values:
    total = total + value
  return total + batch.bias

fn main() -> unit ! (TaskError | IoError):
  batch = Batch {values: [2, 3], bias: 7}
  output: i64 = try parallel:
    gpu = devices.gpu()
    try await spawn sumBatch(batch) on gpu

  try println(format("{}", output))
  return ()
""" }

The loop runs on the GPU. This computes 12 and prints 12.

Supported recursive records and enums can cross the GPU boundary. Records with unsupported fields such as Strings stay on the CPU. You can pass a supported field to a GPU helper, then wrap the returned array in a CPU record:

codeblock { language = kalandra, """from "std/io" import println, IoError
from "std/fmt" import format

type Batch:
  values: [i64]

fn squares(values: [i64]) -> [i64]:
  return generate(length(values), fn(i): return values[i] * values[i])

fn summarize(batch: Batch) -> i64:
  return batch.values[0] + batch.values[1]

fn main() -> unit ! (TaskError | IoError):
  original = Batch {values: [2, 3]}
  output: i64 = try parallel:
    gpu = devices.gpu()
    values = try await spawn squares(original.values) on gpu
    result = Batch {values: values}
    summarize(result)

  try println(format("{}", output))
  return ()
""" }

This computes 13 and prints 13. squares executes on the GPU, while the record construction and summarize execute on the CPU.

This helper returns two fields sharing one fresh array. After await, a write through left is visible through right.

codeblock { language = kalandra, """from "std/io" import println, IoError
from "std/fmt" import format

type Pair:
  left: [i64]
  right: [i64]

fn duplicate(values: [i64]) -> Pair:
  fresh = copy(values)
  return Pair {left: fresh, right: fresh}

fn main() -> unit ! (TaskError | IoError):
  original = [2, 3]
  output: i64 = try parallel:
    result = try await spawn duplicate(original) on devices.gpu()
    result.left[0] = 9
    result.right[0]

  try println(format("{}", output))
  return ()
""" }

This prints 9. The fresh array is independent of original.

h2 { "Pass resident tensors between GPU tasks" }

Transfer a CPU tensor explicitly before passing it to a GPU task. A tensor returned by a GPU task is already resident on that device. Pass it directly to another task on the same device, or transfer it to the CPU when you need a host value.

codeblock { language = kalandra, """from "std/io" import println, IoError
from "std/fmt" import format

fn make(n: i64) -> Tensor[i64]:
  cells = [n]
  return tensor(cells, [])

fn readCell(cell: Tensor[i64]) -> i64:
  return cell[]

fn main() -> unit ! (TaskError | IoError):
  output: i64 = try parallel:
    gpu = devices.gpu()
    cell = try await spawn make(7) on gpu
    try await spawn readCell(cell) on gpu

  try println(format("{}", output))
  return ()
""" }

This prints 7. A rank-zero tensor contains one element and uses empty index brackets. A tensor with a zero extent keeps its rank and dimensions with no elements. Checkpointed helpers can also index, reshape and do elementwise arithmetic on numeric tensors. Their private copies and result materialization use device memory and count toward the task budget.

h2 { "CPU and device language coverage" }

Device support means the operation executes on the selected GPU. A host continuation may schedule GPU work between CPU operations. The portable device column applies to Metal, Vulkan and CUDA, subject to the numerical policies and backend limits above. Windows GPU execution remains experimental and physically unqualified.

