h2 { "Pass a CPU-created callback to the GPU" }

Create a callback on the CPU and pass it to a GPU-placed helper just like another argument. The callback body executes on the GPU. The compiler must know every possible function target and be able to compile each target and its captures for that device.

codeblock { language = kalandra, """from "std/io" import println, IoError
from "std/fmt" import format

fn makeOffset(offset: i64) -> fn(i64) -> i64:
  return fn(value: i64): return value + offset

fn apply(callback: fn(i64) -> i64, value: i64) -> i64:
  return callback(value)

fn main() -> unit ! (TaskError | IoError):
  callback = makeOffset(10)
  output: i64 = try parallel:
    try await spawn apply(callback, 32) on devices.gpu()

  try println(format("{}", output))
  return ()
""" }

This computes 42 and prints 42. makeOffset runs on the CPU; apply and the callback run on the selected GPU. The callback can outlive the function that created it. Transfer does not send CPU machine code or silently execute the callback on the CPU.

Supported captures include numeric and bool scalars, flat numeric and bool arrays, eligible records and enums, CPU tensors of ranks zero through eight, and other eligible closures. Named functions and a compiler-known choice between multiple callbacks also work. Each target must satisfy the selected backend's numerical policy; capturing f64 does not enable hardware f64 arithmetic on a device that lacks it.

Captured arrays are read-only. Their backing and every alias stay read-locked while a task may read them. Await all readers before modifying the original array, or make an independent copy. Passing the same array directly and through a callback preserves its shared identity. Returning captured array backing from the GPU preserves its CPU alias; constructing a new array produces independent storage.

A callback can return supported scalar, array, record, enum or tensor values. Tensor task results stay resident on the GPU and can feed another GPU task. A tensor already resident on the device can be passed as a separate helper argument; it is not a CPU tensor capture. Function values cannot be returned from GPU tasks to the CPU.

Packing and uploading captured data consume time and task/device memory budgets. Prefer small captures and reuse resident tensors for repeated numerical work. Strings and host resources are unavailable in device captures. GPU functions can use declared application errors with supported device payloads. An unknown function target or an unsupported capture is a compile-time error.
